  <div class="prose">
    <p>Medallion is closed to outside investors and is open only to Renaissance's own staff. Deltashark's reading for this strategy: Gregory Zuckerman's <em>The Man Who Solved the Market</em> (2019), on how Renaissance's edge came from a culture of constant research rather than any single model, and Antti Ilmanen's <em>Expected Returns</em> (2011), a dense but rewarding tour of what drives returns across markets.</p>
    <p>For careers: quant firms hire heavily from physics, mathematics, statistics and computer science PhDs, but also software engineers, data engineers and execution specialists. Deltashark suggests working through the stat arb playbook (cointegration tests, spread modelling, factor neutralisation, cost-aware backtests) as the quant equivalent of a stock pitch.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "And in FX, Gold and Bonds?", "How do quants trade my three markets?", "FX, gold and bonds" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>FX: factors work for currencies too</h3>
    <p>The same factor ideas apply to currencies. <strong>Carry</strong> (own high-interest currencies, short low-interest ones), <strong>momentum</strong> (own currencies that have been rising) and <strong>value</strong> (own currencies that look cheap on purchasing power) are the three classic systematic FX strategies. Many quant funds and banks run portfolios of all three, because they tend to fail at different times.</p>
    <h3>Gold: trend and the dollar</h3>
    <p>Systematic funds mostly trade gold through futures, using trend signals (Trend Following covers these in its own note) and its relationships with real interest rates and the dollar. Pairs like gold against silver, or gold miners against gold, are classic stat arb candidates, though their relationships can break for long periods.</p>
    <h3>Bonds: three factors explain the curve</h3>
    <p>Run principal component analysis on daily changes in government bond yields across maturities and a famous result appears: three factors explain almost all the movement. The first is the <strong>level</strong> (all yields up or down together), the second the <strong>slope</strong> (short and long yields moving apart) and the third the <strong>curvature</strong> (the middle moving against the ends). Fixed income quants trade bonds that look rich or cheap after these three are accounted for, a residual approach just like equity stat arb.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 10, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Signal</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A measurable input that predicts returns.</p><p class="eg">"The new signal adds 0.2 to the Sharpe."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Backtest</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Testing a strategy on past data.</p><p class="eg">"Looks great in the backtest. What about live?"</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Out-of-sample</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Data the model never saw while being built.</p><p class="eg">"Hold back 2020 onwards as out-of-sample."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Decay</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A signal's returns fading as others find it.</p><p class="eg">"That signal's decayed to nothing since the paper came out."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Neutralise</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Remove a factor exposure from a portfolio.</p><p class="eg">"Neutralise for sector and beta before you look at it."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Capacity</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>How much money a strategy can run before costs eat the edge.</p><p class="eg">"Great Sharpe, but capacity's only 200 million."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Half-life</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>How long a spread takes to close half its gap.</p><p class="eg">"Half-life's about a week; size it for that."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Factor zoo</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The hundreds of published "factors", most of which don't survive.</p><p class="eg">"Is that real, or another animal from the factor zoo?"</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 11, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where quant thinking turns up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: your own systematic rules</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Every strategy you code in Pine Script or Python is a small quant pipeline: data, signal, sizing, execution, risk. The overfitting interactive is the most important lesson for it. Write down every variation you test, keep a stretch of data you never look at until the end, and prefer simple rules with a reason behind them, such as carry or trend in FX, over a tuned rule with ten settings.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: valuation by model</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Property has its own quant tools. Automated valuation models price a home from its size, location, bedrooms and recent sales nearby, the same factor thinking applied to houses. For villas, a simple model of nightly rates against location, size, pool and distance to the beach shows which properties are cheap relative to what they could earn.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: alternative data where data is scarce</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The Nigerian Exchange is too small and thinly traded for large quant funds: a few stocks dominate, and trading costs would swallow most signals. But quant thinking is thriving in Nigerian fintech, where lenders score borrowers using alternative data such as mobile money and phone usage instead of credit histories most people don't have. The same disciplines apply: test out-of-sample, and watch for relationships that break when conditions change.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 12, "The Maths Underneath", "What are the key formulas behind quant strategies?" %}
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">The multi-factor model</span>\[ r_i = \alpha_i + \sum_k \beta_{i,k}\, f_k + \varepsilon_i \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">The cointegrated spread and its z-score</span>\[ Z_t = Y_t - \beta X_t \qquad z_t = \frac{Z_t - \mu}{\sigma_Z} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Mean reversion and the half-life (Ornstein–Uhlenbeck)</span>\[ dZ_t = -\theta\,(Z_t - \mu)\,dt + \sigma\, dW_t \qquad \text{half-life} = \frac{\ln 2}{\theta} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">How lucky can a backtest look?</span>\[ \text{SE}(\widehat{SR}) \approx \frac{1}{\sqrt{T}} \qquad \text{best of } N \text{ noise Sharpes} \lesssim \frac{\sqrt{2 \ln N}}{\sqrt{T}} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>\(f_k\) are factor returns and \(\beta_{i,k}\) the share's loadings on them; \(\varepsilon_i\) is the residual that stat arb trades. \(\theta\) is the speed of mean reversion. \(T\) is years of data and \(N\) the number of rules tried; the second is a rough upper guide to how good the luckiest of \(N\) useless rules will look (the interactive uses a more precise version).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example</span>
    <h3>A researcher tests \(400\) rules on \(4\) years of data. The best shows a Sharpe ratio of \(1.6\). How impressed should she be?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Uncertainty in any one Sharpe: \(1 \div \sqrt{4} = 0.5\).</li>
      <li>Best of \(400\) pure-noise rules: up to about \(\sqrt{2 \ln 400} \div \sqrt{4} = \sqrt{12.0} \div 2 \approx 1.7\); more precisely, typically around \(1.4\).</li>
      <li>Her best, \(1.6\), is within the range luck alone produces.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Not at all. She needs out-of-sample results, or a much smaller number of well-motivated ideas.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 13, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>Name the five steps of the systematic investment pipeline, and who works on each.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Data (data engineers), alpha or signal research (quant researchers), portfolio construction (portfolio managers, optimisers), execution (execution quants, developers) and risk (risk quants).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>Pick two factors and give the two competing explanations for why each pays.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Value: a risk reward (cheap firms are more fragile) or a bias (investors overreact to bad news). Momentum: a bias (investors underreact, then herd) or, less convincingly, risk (momentum can crash suddenly, as in 2009). Low volatility: lottery preference and investors who can't borrow to buy safe shares.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>A spread has a mean-reversion speed of \(\theta = 0.07\) per day. What's its half-life, and how long would you expect to hold a trade?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(\ln 2 \div 0.07 \approx 9.9\) days, so roughly two weeks per trade.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>Give two ways a stat arb strategy fails.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Regime change, where the relationship breaks (a merger, a restatement, an industry shift) and the spread never comes back; and signal decay, where crowding by firms using similar methods shrinks the returns.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>What did McLean and Pontiff find, and what does it mean for quant funds?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Across \(97\) published anomalies, returns fell by about \(58\%\) on average after publication. Public signals get crowded and turned into cheap factor products, so lasting edge has to come from continuous research into new, unpublished signals.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">6</span><span>Interview question (Deltashark): explain the August 2007 quant quake and what it reveals.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>A large quant fund de-leveraged fast, selling positions many other quant funds held because of similar models. That pushed prices against all of them, triggering more de-leveraging in a self-reinforcing spiral; some funds lost \(10\)–\(30\%\) in a week. It showed that market-neutral strategies are still exposed to crowding, that liquidity risk can cause losses unrelated to the merit of positions, and that historical correlations break down under stress.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">7</span><span>Interview question (Deltashark): what is overfitting, why is it the main risk for systematic strategies, and how do firms fight it?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Overfitting is tuning a model to past data so well that it captures noise rather than repeatable signal, so it disappoints live. It's the main risk because financial data is noisy, changes over time and is fought over by other traders, and because testing many ideas guarantees some look good by luck. Defences: out-of-sample and walk-forward testing on data the model never saw, and requiring an economic reason (a behavioural bias or a structural constraint) for why a signal should persist. Counting how many ideas were tried and penalising for it helps too.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}
